Print NULL %s arguments as (null) on QNX.

QNX's printf family dereferences a NULL "%s" argument (hazard 99) where
glibc prints "(null)", and Samba's debug and error messages print optional
strings that way. QNX's libc has a hook for this: when the variable
output_for_percent_s_NULL points to a string, that string is printed
instead. libreplace, which every Samba program and library links, sets it
from a constructor (the QNX ports' Samba 4.11 defined it in smbd and nmbd).

Upstream-Status: QNX-specific

--- a/lib/replace/replace.c
+++ b/lib/replace/replace.c
@@ -1395,3 +1395,16 @@
 
 const char hexchars_lower[] = "0123456789abcdef";
 const char hexchars_upper[] = "0123456789ABCDEF";
+
+#ifdef __QNX__
+/*
+ * QNX's printf family dereferences a NULL "%s" argument (and dies) where
+ * glibc prints "(null)"; Samba's debug and error messages rely on the
+ * latter. QNX's libc prints this string instead when the variable is set.
+ */
+extern const char *output_for_percent_s_NULL;
+static void __attribute__((constructor)) rep_qnx_printf_null(void)
+{
+	output_for_percent_s_NULL = "(null)";
+}
+#endif
